---
import FitBadge from './FitBadge.astro';
import FitBars from './FitBars.astro';
import Mark from './Mark.astro';
import {
  ABSENCE,
  AGE_BASIS_LABEL,
  ageOf,
  applyLabel,
  compShort,
  compTop,
  easeSegments,
  facetsOf,
  firstSeen,
  firstSeenVisible,
  formatAge,
  formatDate,
  locationShort,
  markStateOf,
  sourceLabel,
  sweptStamp,
  workplaceOf,
  type Job,
  type SortRanks
} from '../lib/data';
import { jobPath } from '../data/nav';

/**
 * One row of the index table, to the approved canvas: fit with its why
 * expander, the role as posted with its company and mark, comp as posted,
 * location, age, apply.
 *
 * WHAT A ROW PROMISES.
 *
 * Every cell is the source's own words or a number the machine measured. The
 * title is not tidied, the location keeps its hedges ("SF or NYC, city
 * unconfirmed"), and the comp string keeps its equity notes and zone
 * qualifiers, because "as posted" is the column heading and a cleaned up value
 * would make this table a summary of postings rather than a record of them.
 *
 * ONE NAMED EXCEPTION. The location cell renders locationDisplay(job.location)
 * rather than job.location itself: a redundant "United States" is dropped
 * from what is DISPLAYED, never from the stored field, the facet a filter
 * reads, or anything else that touches the record. See that function's own
 * header in src/lib/data.ts for the full argument; the short version is that
 * naming the reader's own country adds no information a US reader did not
 * already have, so removing it changes no meaning, and every other country is
 * left exactly as posted because removing one of those would.
 *
 * Where the source gave nothing, the cell says so in the human voice: "Not
 * listed" and "No date shown" are editorial statements about our knowledge, not
 * machine readings, so they are never set in mono. That is standing rule 3 doing
 * real work: a reader can tell a measurement from an absence by the typeface
 * alone, before reading a word.
 *
 * THE AGE CELL carries its own basis. An age computed from the date the source
 * published gets "Posted <date>"; an age computed from the sweep we first saw
 * it gets "First seen <date>", and only ever on rows where that date is not
 * today. Rows with no date to count from show the absence, which on this sweep
 * is five of the 37. See DECISIONS.md, "The age column: two different facts".
 *
 * THE VERIFICATION LINE is the scoped label MASTER-SPEC F10 asks every row to
 * carry: which feed the posting was observed on, and when we last verified it.
 * It used to live only in the Mark's aria-label, which a sighted reader on
 * this row never saw: a screen reader announced "Verified against the
 * Greenhouse source" and everyone else got a colored glyph with no partner.
 * That also meant a row rendered anywhere other than inside JobTable, which is
 * the only place that prints the sweep stamp as a page-level banner, carried
 * no verification statement of its own at all. Standing rule 5 says a mark
 * never stands alone, so the Mark below is passed no `label`: the visible line
 * is the text partner now, said once, in one shape, on every row regardless of
 * status or where the row is mounted. Re-verified status used to live a second
 * place too, nested inside the age cell's `age &&` branch, so a re-verified
 * row with no measurable age silently dropped the word "re-verified"
 * altogether. Stating it here, unconditionally, is the fix: one row, one
 * verification fact, in one place.
 *
 * THE DATA ATTRIBUTES are the filter and sort island's whole interface. Writing
 * the facets here means filtering never has to ship the data to the browser: the
 * rows already know which buckets they are in, and the controls only have to
 * read attributes. Nothing here is decorative, so nothing here is optional.
 */

interface Props {
  job: Job;
  /** Which surface the row sits on, passed to the mark for its knockout. */
  surface?: 'page' | 'raised' | 'hover';
  /**
   * Where the title links. Defaults to jobPath (the /role page the design set
   * prerenders). The unified board passes its own resolver so a tracked row
   * links to its /board page instead, without this component knowing which
   * surface it is on.
   */
  hrefFor?: (job: Job) => string;
  /**
   * This row's position under each of the three sorts, from sortRanks().
   * Supplied by JobTable when the sort control is an island: the browser then
   * orders rows by an integer instead of re-deriving what "sort by comp" means.
   */
  ranks?: SortRanks;
  /** Pre-hidden because it belongs to a later page. Keeps the first paint and
      the island's first state identical, so nothing moves on load. */
  hidden?: boolean;
  /**
   * Whether this reader sees the fit score. False drops the fit cell and its
   * why panel from the markup (flags.config.mjs fit_public, dark for a
   * signed-out reader); the data attributes stay, because they describe the
   * row and not who is looking at it.
   */
  fit?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const { job, surface = 'raised', ranks, hidden = false, fit = true, class: className, hrefFor } = Astro.props;
const titleHref = hrefFor ? hrefFor(job) : jobPath(job.slug);

const age = ageOf(job);
const observed = firstSeen(job);
const facets = facetsOf(job);
const panelId = `why-${job.slug}`;
const isClosed = job.status === 'closed';
const top = compTop(job);
const place = locationShort(job.location);
// The verification, on the mark's accessible label rather than a visible line.
const verificationLabel = `${
  isClosed ? 'Closed, confirmed' : job.status === 're_verified' ? 'Re-verified' : 'Verified'
} on the ${sourceLabel(job)} source, ${sweptStamp()}`;
---

<li
  class:list={['job-row', isClosed && 'job-row-closed', className]}
  data-job-row
  data-slug={job.slug}
  data-company={job.company}
  data-status={job.status}
  data-fit={job.fit.total}
  data-comp-top={top === null ? '' : top}
  data-age={age === null ? '' : age.days}
  data-facet-location={facets.location}
  data-facet-comp={facets.comp}
  data-facet-freshness={facets.freshness}
  data-facet-stage={facets.stage}
  data-rank-fit={ranks?.fit}
  data-rank-comp={ranks?.comp}
  data-rank-age={ranks?.age}
  hidden={hidden}
>
  {fit && (
    <div class="cell cell-fit">
      <span class="visually-hidden">Fit score</span>
      <FitBadge job={job} />
      {/*
        The expander is a real button with a real aria-expanded, and it controls a
        panel that already exists in the markup. JobTable ships a noscript rule
        that opens every panel and hides every toggle, so a reader without
        scripting gets the reasoning rather than a control that does nothing.
      */}
      <button class="why" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls={panelId} data-why-toggle>
        why
        <span class="why-caret" aria-hidden="true">&#9662;</span>
      </button>
    </div>
  )}

  <div class="cell cell-role">
    <span class="visually-hidden">Role, as posted</span>
    {
      /*
        TITLE FIRST. The role a person is scanning for is the headline of the
        row, so it leads the cell, with the company and the verification receipt
        below it. This is the reading order a job board is expected to have; the
        machine's provenance (which board, verified when) is the fine print that
        earns the headline, not the other way round.

        A pre-posting row has no title and gets no link, because there is no
        role page to link to and no posting to name. It says what is true, in
        the human voice the table already uses for an absence, and then says
        what the machine did observe: how many roles that company has open and
        that none of them is a design role. That sentence is the whole reason
        the row is on the list.
      */
      job.title !== null && titleHref ? (
        <a class="title" href={titleHref}>
          {job.title}
        </a>
      ) : (
        <span class="title-absent">
          <span class="absence">No role posted yet</span>
          {job.prospect?.one_liner && <span class="one-liner">{job.prospect.one_liner}</span>}
        </span>
      )
    }
    <span class="row-source">
      {/* The verification travels on the mark's own label now, not a visible
          line per row. Every row carried "Observed on <source>, verified <the
          same sweep stamp>", which repeated the top banner's stamp on every row
          and named a source the Apply button already names. It clutters the
          overview and belongs on the detail page, so the row keeps the mark and
          hands a screen reader the full statement through its label (standing
          rule 5: the mark still never stands without its meaning). */}
      <Mark state={markStateOf(job)} surface={surface} label={verificationLabel} />
      <span class="company mono">{job.company}</span>
      {/*
        THE APPLIED TAG (client side, like the seen dimmer beside it in
        Filters.astro). Always in the markup, hidden by CSS by default: the
        index is a prerendered static page and cannot know at build time
        which reader is looking at it or what they have applied to, so there
        is no server-rendered state to branch on here. Filters.astro's own
        script already fetches this reader's applied set from GET
        /settings/filters to run applied suppression; once it knows the
        answer it sets data-applied on this row's own <li> (the same shape
        it already uses for data-seen-dimmed), and the sibling selector
        below is the only thing that ever reveals this span. Text, not a bare
        colour swatch, so the fact survives for a screen reader and for a
        reader who cannot distinguish the one accent colour this
        monochrome site allows itself from the page around it.
      */}
      <span class="applied-tag" data-applied-tag>Applied</span>
    </span>
  </div>

  <div class="cell cell-comp">
    <span class="visually-hidden">Pay</span>
    {/* The clean range only. A posting that writes its pay as a page of
        location-conditional bands would break this cell, so the row shows the
        one number from the structured range and the full text lives on the
        detail page. See compShort() in src/lib/data.ts. */}
    {
      compShort(job) ? (
        <span class="comp mono">{compShort(job)}</span>
      ) : (
        <span class="absence">{ABSENCE.value}</span>
      )
    }
  </div>

  <div class="cell cell-location">
    <span class="visually-hidden">Location</span>
    {/* The primary place only, with a "+N more" when the posting lists
        several. locationShort() (src/lib/data.ts) drops the redundant home
        country and regroups a run of comma-separated cities, so a posting
        listing a dozen locations does not break the row. The full list is on
        the detail page. job.location itself is never touched, so the facet the
        filter reads still sees the employer's own string. */}
    <span class="location mono">
      {place.primary}
      {place.more > 0 && <span class="location-more"> +{place.more} more</span>}
    </span>
    {
      /* How the role is worked, under the where. Remote and On-site come from
         the posting's own remote flag; Hybrid appears only when the employer's
         location text says so. See workplaceOf() in src/lib/data.ts for why
         this is not a free three-way and why On-site is a default, not an
         observed fact. A pre-posting row returns null and renders nothing. */
      workplaceOf(job) && <span class="workplace mono-label">{workplaceOf(job)}</span>
    }
  </div>

  <div class="cell cell-age">
    <span class="visually-hidden">Age</span>
    {
      age ? (
        <span class="age-line">
          <span class="age mono" data-truth="machine">{formatAge(job)}</span>
        </span>
      ) : (
        <span class="absence">{ABSENCE.date}</span>
      )
    }

    {/* The basis, so the number above it is never a figure a reader has to take
        on trust. Rendered only where there is a number to explain. */}
    {
      age && (
        <span class="age-basis mono-label">
          {AGE_BASIS_LABEL[age.basis]} {formatDate(age.from)}
        </span>
      )
    }

    {/* The First seen column, built and gated on an observation rather than on a
        count of nights. It stays dark while most rows were first seen on the
        sweep that rendered them, because a column of identical dates would imply
        every posting appeared overnight. Once the archive is deeper than the
        sweep it becomes the column no competitor has, since it comes from our
        archive rather than from the employer. The gate used to be
        stats.sweep_number >= 3; that field was dropped on 2026-08-19 and the
        proxy went with it. */}
    {
      firstSeenVisible() && observed && (
        <span class="age-basis mono-label">First seen {formatDate(observed)}</span>
      )
    }
  </div>

  <div class="cell cell-apply">
    <span class="visually-hidden">Apply</span>
    {
      job.kind === 'pre_posting' ? (
        /* Nothing to apply to, so the cell does not offer one. The link goes to
           the company's own profile, labelled for what it is. Writing "Apply"
           here would be the site inventing an application flow that does not
           exist, which is the same objection as inventing a title. */
        <>
          <span class="absence">No posting to apply to</span>
          {job.prospect?.profile_url && (
            <a class="apply-link" href={job.prospect.profile_url} rel="noopener nofollow">
              See the company
              <span aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span>
            </a>
          )}
        </>
      ) : isClosed ? (
        /* No apply path anywhere on a closed record. The row states what
           happened and links to the page that holds the history. */
        <>
          <span class="ease mono">Closed{job.closed_reason ? `, ${job.closed_reason.toLowerCase()}` : ''}</span>
          <a class="apply-link" href={jobPath(job.slug)}>See the record</a>
        </>
      ) : (
        <>
          {/* One element per fact, so a narrow column breaks the line between
              facts and never inside one. As a single string this wrapped as
              "no" / "account", which reads like two things. */}
          <span class="ease mono">
            {easeSegments(job).map((part, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 && <span class="ease-sep" aria-hidden="true"> &#183; </span>}
                <span class="ease-part">{part}</span>
              </>
            ))}
          </span>
          <a class="apply-link" href={job.apply_url} rel="noopener nofollow">
            {applyLabel(job)}
            <span aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span>
          </a>
        </>
      )
    }
  </div>

  {fit && (
    <div class="why-panel" id={panelId} hidden data-why-panel>
      <FitBars job={job} heading={`Why ${job.fit.total}`} />
    </div>
  )}
</li>

<style>
  .job-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: var(--table-columns);
    gap: var(--space-400);
    align-items: center;
    padding: var(--space-300) var(--space-600);
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
    border-bottom: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-line);
    /* The knockout on a re-verified mark is filled with the row's real
       background, and the row has two. Setting the property here and again on
       hover means the mark follows the row without knowing what a row is. */
    --mark-knockout-fill: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  /* `display: grid` above outranks the browser's own [hidden] rule, which is at
     the same specificity and comes earlier, so a hidden row would stay on
     screen. This is the fix, and it has to live beside the grid declaration
     that caused it. */
  .job-row[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .job-row:hover {
    background: var(--color-hover-surface);
    --mark-knockout-fill: var(--color-hover-surface);
  }

  /* Focus inside a row lifts the whole row, so a keyboard reader sees the same
     context a cursor reader does rather than a single lit link in a flat list. */
  .job-row:focus-within {
    background: var(--color-hover-surface);
    --mark-knockout-fill: var(--color-hover-surface);
  }

  .cell {
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-100);
  }

  .cell-fit {
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .why {
    font-family: var(--font-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--size-050);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    border-bottom: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
    padding-block: var(--space-100);
  }

  .why:hover {
    color: var(--color-foreground);
    border-bottom-color: var(--color-foreground);
  }

  /* The caret turns to point down when the panel is open. A transform, not a
     second glyph, so the character in the accessibility tree never changes. */
  .why-caret {
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .why[aria-expanded='true'] .why-caret {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  .row-source {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-200);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .company {
    font-size: var(--size-075);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }

  /* THE APPLIED TAG. Off by default, and revealed only once this row's own
     <li> carries data-applied, which Filters.astro's script sets from the
     account's suppressedSlugs set (see this element's own comment in the
     markup above). This is the one accent colour a monochrome site allows
     itself: "applied" is a state a reader put the row into, not a
     decoration this site chose, and --color-mark-live is the live/verified
     green the row's own Mark and verification line already carry that
     meaning in, reused here rather than a new hex value invented for one
     tag. Sharp corners, not a pill: --radius is this site's one rounding
     value everywhere else, and a badge that rounds further than a button or
     a chip would be the one shape on the page nothing else uses. */
  .applied-tag {
    display: none;
    font-family: var(--font-family-sans);
    font-size: var(--size-050);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    color: var(--color-foreground-inverse);
    background: var(--color-mark-live);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-100) var(--space-200);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .job-row[data-applied] .applied-tag {
    display: inline-block;
  }

  .title {
    font-size: var(--size-200);
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .title:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: var(--text-underline-offset);
  }

  .comp,
  .age {
    font-size: var(--size-075);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-feature-settings: var(--feature-tabular);
  }

  /* The age is one short machine reading and always fits its track, so it holds
     one line. The comp is the source's own sentence and does not, so it wraps
     between parts and never inside one. Two rows on this sweep need the second
     line; the rest are unchanged, and a row was already free to grow because a
     long title wraps in the column beside it. */
  .age {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .comp {
    white-space: normal;
  }

  .comp-part {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .location {
    font-size: var(--size-075);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* The "+N more" hint after the primary place. Lower case and dimmer, so it
     reads as a count of what is on the detail page rather than another place. */
  .location-more {
    text-transform: none;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  /* The workplace label under the location. Same muted small-caps register the
     verification line and age basis already use for a machine reading, so it
     reads as fine print about the where rather than a second headline. */
  .workplace {
    display: block;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-100);
  }

  .age-line {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .age-basis {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* An absence is editorial, so it is never mono. A reader can tell a
     measurement from a gap by the typeface before reading the words. */
  .absence {
    font-size: var(--size-100);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* The role cell on a pre-posting row: the absence, then the company's own
     one line description of itself, which is the closest thing to a role
     description that exists before a role does. */
  .title-absent {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-100);
  }
  .one-liner {
    font-size: var(--size-100);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* Each fact holds together; the line breaks between them. */
  .ease-part {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .ease {
    font-size: var(--size-050);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .apply-link {
    font-size: var(--size-100);
    border-bottom: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-foreground);
    align-self: flex-start;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .apply-link:hover {
    background: var(--color-foreground);
    color: var(--color-foreground-inverse);
  }

  /* The reasoning panel spans every column. It is in the markup at all times and
     hidden by the `hidden` attribute, which is what lets the noscript rule in
     JobTable reveal it without a script ever running. */
  .why-panel {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    padding-top: var(--space-300);
    border-top: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-line);
    max-width: var(--layout-measure);
  }

  /* A closed row keeps its place in the record and loses its emphasis. Grey,
     never red: a closed posting is an honest archive entry, and the loss colour
     belongs to statistics. */
  .job-row-closed .title {
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .job-row,
    .why,
    .apply-link,
    .title {
      transition:
        background-color var(--motion-duration-instant) var(--motion-easing-standard),
        border-color var(--motion-duration-instant) var(--motion-easing-standard),
        color var(--motion-duration-instant) var(--motion-easing-standard);
    }
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Phone. The row becomes the card the 375px canvas draws: the company and its
     mark with the score opposite, the title at reading size under them, comp
     and location on one line, then the age and the apply action.

     Named grid areas rather than source order, because the DOM order is the
     reading order a screen reader gets at both widths and it should not change
     to suit a layout.
     --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  @media (max-width: 60rem) { /* --breakpoint-lap */
    .job-row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
      grid-template-areas:
        'role fit'
        'comp location'
        'age age'
        'apply apply'
        'why why';
      gap: var(--space-200) var(--space-400);
      padding: var(--space-400);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .cell-fit {
      grid-area: fit;
      align-items: flex-end;
    }
    .cell-role {
      grid-area: role;
    }
    .cell-comp {
      grid-area: comp;
    }
    .cell-location {
      grid-area: location;
    }
    .cell-age {
      grid-area: age;
    }
    .cell-apply {
      grid-area: apply;
    }
    .why-panel {
      grid-area: why;
    }

    .title {
      font-size: var(--size-300);
    }

    .location {
      white-space: nowrap;
      overflow: hidden;
      text-overflow: ellipsis;
    }

    .apply-link {
      /* A thumb target, and the padding is vertical only so the underline still
         sits tight under the words. */
      padding-block: var(--space-200);
    }
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Phone proper. The card above pairs comp and location on one line, and that
     pairing cannot hold at phone widths. It was measured, not guessed:

       The two tracks are `1fr auto`. `auto` is a max-content track, so it is
       sized by the WIDEST thing in the right-hand column, and on this sweep
       that is a location reading "SF or NYC, 4 days in office, city
       unconfirmed", whose max-content is 388.88px. On a 414px phone the whole
       card is 348px wide, so that one track claims more than the card has, the
       `1fr` track collapses toward zero, and `.cell`'s `min-width: 0` (which
       the six-column desktop table needs, so it stays) means the starved
       column does not push back: its text simply paints outside its own box.

       The result is not "tight". It is overprinted. At 414px the salary
       "$172k-$258k" is drawn directly on top of "FULLY REMOTE IF BEYOND
       COMMUTE OF S...", and both become unreadable. The same happens at 320,
       375 and 480. Row 1 of sweep 001 happens to have a short location, which
       is why the first screen looks fine and page 2 does not.

     So below the phone breakpoint every fact takes the full card width. This is
     what --breakpoint-phone's own description already promises ("every
     multi-column band stacks") and it is the only arrangement that is safe for
     any location string a source can post, rather than safe for the strings
     this sweep happened to return.

     The location also drops its ellipsis here. On one shared line truncation
     was the lesser evil; on its own line there is nothing to truncate for, and
